# Exercise 4: Difference Between JPA, Hibernate and Spring Data JPA

## Objective

To understand the differences between JPA, Hibernate, and Spring Data JPA, and how they work together in a Spring Boot application.

---

# 1. JPA (Java Persistence API)

JPA stands for **Java Persistence API**.

It is a **Java specification** that defines a standard way to map Java objects to relational databases.

JPA itself is **not a framework** and does not perform database operations. It only defines rules and interfaces that ORM frameworks must implement.

### Features

- Standard Java persistence specification
- Uses annotations for object-relational mapping
- Database independent
- Supports CRUD operations
- Uses EntityManager for database interaction

### Common JPA Annotations

- @Entity
- @Table
- @Id
- @Column
- @GeneratedValue
- @OneToMany
- @ManyToOne
- @JoinColumn

### Example

```java
@Entity
@Table(name="country")
public class Country {

    @Id
    @Column(name="co_code")
    private String code;

    @Column(name="co_name")
    private String name;

}
```

---

# 2. Hibernate

Hibernate is an **Object Relational Mapping (ORM) Framework**.

It is one of the most popular implementations of the JPA specification.

Hibernate converts Java objects into database records and database records back into Java objects.

### Features

- Implements JPA
- Automatic SQL generation
- Session management
- Transaction management
- Caching support
- Lazy and eager loading
- HQL (Hibernate Query Language)

### Example

```java
Session session = sessionFactory.openSession();

Transaction transaction = session.beginTransaction();

Country country = session.get(Country.class, "IN");

transaction.commit();

session.close();
```

In Hibernate, developers manually manage sessions and transactions.

---

# 3. Spring Data JPA

Spring Data JPA is a **Spring Framework module** built on top of JPA.

It reduces boilerplate code by automatically generating repository implementations.

Developers only need to create repository interfaces.

### Features

- Built on JPA
- Automatic CRUD methods
- Pagination
- Sorting
- Derived Query Methods
- JPQL Support
- Native SQL Support
- Easy integration with Spring Boot

### Example Repository

```java
@Repository
public interface CountryRepository extends JpaRepository<Country,String>{

}
```

Spring automatically provides methods like:

```java
findAll()

findById()

save()

deleteById()

count()

existsById()
```

---

# Example Service

```java
@Service
public class CountryService {

    @Autowired
    CountryRepository repository;

    public List<Country> getAllCountries(){

        return repository.findAll();

    }

}
```

---

# Comparison Table

| Feature | JPA | Hibernate | Spring Data JPA |
|----------|-----|-----------|-----------------|
| Type | Specification | ORM Framework | Spring Module |
| Developed By | Oracle | Hibernate Team | Spring Team |
| Purpose | Defines persistence standards | Implements JPA | Simplifies database access |
| Implementation | No | Yes | Uses JPA implementation |
| CRUD Support | Through EntityManager | Through Session | Automatically provided |
| SQL Generation | No | Yes | Yes |
| Repository Support | No | No | Yes |
| Boilerplate Code | Medium | High | Very Low |
| Transaction Management | EntityManager | Hibernate Transaction | Spring Transaction |
| Query Support | JPQL | HQL, JPQL | Derived Queries, JPQL, Native SQL |

